{"archetypes":[{"name":"atomicity-rollback","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The preserve_and_publish_ns_retry function performs a multi-step atomic publish (cp first-pass, mktemp, cp retry, mv) with rollback on failure; verify every failure branch correctly cleans up both the sidecar and the temp file, and that no partial state is left behind in signal-interrupted or concurrent scenarios.","prompt_body":"Review `preserve_and_publish_ns_retry` in `scripts/collect-agent-results.sh` for atomic publish correctness. Specifically:\n1. Verify the EXIT-trap or manual cleanup in every failure branch removes exactly the right files (no leaked temp file, no orphaned first-pass sidecar) under: cp-first-pass fails, mktemp fails, cp-retry fails, mv fails.\n2. Check whether the `mv -f` of the publish_tmp to orig_output is truly atomic on the same filesystem; if mktemp could land on a different filesystem (e.g. $TMPDIR is a ramdisk and orig_output is on a network share), atomicity breaks — flag the assumption.\n3. Confirm that if the process receives a signal between 'cp first-pass succeeds' and 'mv retry to orig', the sidecar is not orphaned (it would be, because there is no EXIT trap in the function itself — only at the script level).\n4. Review the structured-sidecar copy after preserve_and_publish_ns_retry returns: if that cp fails the result record still points at the retry-path sidecar; confirm this is deliberate and safe.\n5. Check whether `rm -f \"$publish_tmp\" \"$first_pass_sidecar\"` in the failure branch could accidentally clobber a pre-existing file at those paths."},{"name":"result-record-consistency","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"After the publish, RESULTS[IDX] is rewritten to REVIEWER_FILE=$ORIG_OUTPUT; downstream consumers that previously keyed on the ns-retry path must consistently receive the updated path and sidecar location — verify field ordering, STRUCTURED_SIDECAR updates, and that no stale ns-retry path leaks into emitted output.","prompt_body":"Audit the RESULTS[IDX] update sites in the NS-retry success branches of `scripts/collect-agent-results.sh`:\n1. Confirm the structured branch sets REVIEWER_FILE=$ORIG_OUTPUT (not $NS_RETRY_OUTPUT) and STRUCTURED_SIDECAR to the new orig-path sidecar after the cp, falling back to the retry-path sidecar if cp fails — and that this fallback value still names a file that actually exists on disk.\n2. Confirm the substantive branch similarly uses REVIEWER_FILE=$ORIG_OUTPUT.\n3. Check that `emit_summary_result` in Section 4 handles the STRUCTURED_SIDECAR field correctly for both the 'cp succeeded' and 'cp failed (fallback)' paths — field ordering in the pipe-delimited record must be stable.\n4. Verify there is no code path where RESULTS[IDX] still references $NS_RETRY_OUTPUT after the new publish logic."}]}
